                <pb facs="0602" n="590" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg590"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/13299: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-07-19T19:00:00+03:00">July 19, 1941—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 20—3:27 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>981. My June 7, 3 p.m. With reference to an item released by Reuter yesterday
                    regarding renewed negotiations for the evacuation of French women and children
                    from French Somaliland, the Legation learns from a British Embassy source that
                    negotiations along these lines are taking place. Furthermore it is stated that
                    the situation in Djibouti has shown marked signs of deterioration recently and
                    that an early collapse would not be surprising. British hopes in this respect
                    are somewhat offset, however, by the fear that, before finally giving in, the
                    French authorities will blow up the harbor and railway facilities at
                    Djibouti.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
